{0 Getting started}

Build a Cloudflare Worker in OCaml, run it locally, then change its response.
Start outside the workero source checkout so Dune treats your Worker as its
own project. Deployment is optional and needs a Cloudflare account.

{1 Prerequisites}

- {{:https://opam.ocaml.org/doc/Install.html}opam} with an active OCaml 5.4 or
  later switch. The source package requires Dune 3.24 or later,
  [js_of_ocaml] and [wasm_of_ocaml-compiler] 6.5.0 or later and below 6.6,
  and [jsonkit] 1.1 or later and below 2. opam resolves these dependencies.
- Git to clone workero.
- Node.js 22 or later. The opam [wrangler] package runs on it.
- binaryen 119 or later, so that [wasm-opt] is on [PATH] for wasm_of_ocaml.
  Run [npm install -g binaryen] or build it from
  {{:https://github.com/WebAssembly/binaryen}source}.

The [workero] package is not yet available from the opam package index. Install
it from source instead.

{1 1. Create a project}

From a directory outside any Dune project, clone workero and install its
package into your active switch. Copy the template into a sibling directory:

{@sh[
eval "$(opam env)"
git clone https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero.git workero-source
opam pin add workero ./workero-source -y
opam install wrangler -y
cp -R workero-source/template my-worker
cd my-worker
]}

The pin installs the [workero] library and CLI. [opam install wrangler]
installs Wrangler and [workerd] into the switch. Keep [my-worker] outside
[workero-source] so Dune does not select the source checkout as the project
root. See
{{!page-template}The project template} for the files in your copy.

{1 2. Run locally}

Start Wrangler from [my-worker]:

{@sh[
opam exec -- wrangler dev
]}

Wrangler runs [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js], packages the Worker in
[_worker/], and prints a local URL, usually [http://127.0.0.1:8787]. Leave
the server running. In another terminal, request the template's routes:

{@sh[
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/
curl -d ping http://127.0.0.1:8787/echo
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/env
]}

Expect [Hello from OCaml on Cloudflare Workers], [ping], and
[hello from vars], in that order. The third value comes from [[vars]] in
[wrangler.toml]. Local development does not require Cloudflare login.

{1 3. Change the home page}

Open [src/main.ml] and change the [GET, "/"] branch to:

{@ocaml[
| GET, "/" -> Response.text "Hello from my worker\n"
]}

Wrangler watches [src/] and rebuilds after you save. Request the root path
again:

{@sh[
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/
]}

Expect [Hello from my worker]. The template registers the handler with
{!Workero.serve}. Continue with {{!page-runtime_apis}Runtime APIs} for HTTP,
JSON, and handlers, or {{!page-bindings}Bindings} for Cloudflare resources.

{1 4. Deploy (optional)}

If you have a Cloudflare account and want a public Worker, authenticate and
deploy from [my-worker]:

{@sh[
opam exec -- wrangler login
opam exec -- wrangler deploy
]}

Wrangler prints the public URL. It may ask you to set up a [workers.dev]
subdomain on your first deployment. Request that URL to see
[Hello from my worker]. See {{!page-cli}CLI} for packaging details.
